// The ICML style — the TEMPLATE FAMILY of the International Conference on Machine Learning:
// everything the style file in ICML's author kit decides, whatever the edition. A venue preset
// extends it ("extends": "paperlint:icml-style") and adds what its call for papers sets: the page
// limit of each kind of paper, and the sections it requires. The 2027 edition is icml-2027.jsonc.
//
// The template is not a class. The author kit
// (https://media.icml.cc/Conferences/ICML2027/styles/icml2027.zip, read 2026-11-19:
// example_paper.tex, icml2027.sty «version of 2027-10-30», icml2027.bst, and its own copies of
// fancyhdr.sty, algorithm.sty and algorithmic.sty) opens with `\documentclass{article}` and loads
// `\usepackage{icml2027}` (`[accepted]` for the camera-ready) from a style file the author copies
// beside paper.tex. The style sets the page itself (`\paperwidth=8.5in`, `\textwidth{6.75in}`,
// `\textheight{9.0in}`, `\columnsep{0.25in}`, `\twocolumn`) and Times (`\RequirePackage{times}`),
// and the kit's example says what it sets: «The text of the paper should be formatted in two
// columns, with an overall width of 6.75 inches, height of 9.0 inches, and 0.25 inches between the
// columns.», «all final versions must be produced for US letter size.», «Your paper should be in 10
// point Times font.»
//
// 🔴 NO `template` YET, ON PURPOSE. The class is plain `article`: as a template alone it would pass
// any article paper, with or without the style (measured: a paper in NeurIPS 2025's style passed
// tex/template under `\documentclass{article}`; only pdf/geometry's column count caught it). The
// contract for the family field that names the style, being added with the NeurIPS family, is
//   "template": "\\documentclass{article}",
//   "template_style": "\\usepackage{icml2027}",
// with no option, since the call requires none for the submission. They go here when the schema
// takes `template_style`; today it refuses the key.
//
// 🔴 THE NUMBERS ARE MEASURED, NOT COPIED. Built on 2026-11-19 with `paperlint build` on paperlint's
// TeX Live 2026 (pdfTeX 1.40.29) and banal: the kit's own example_paper.tex with its style, one line
// added — `\crefname{assumption}{Assumption}{Assumptions}`: without it the kit's example does not
// build («There were undefined references»: TeX Live 2026's cleveref has no format for its
// `assumption` theorem; the kit's PDF, made with pdfTeX 1.40.25, prints «Assumption 2.6»). The
// style's dimensions were written to the log by a `\typeout` at \begin{document}:
//
//   page (banal)          8.5 x 11 in                 \paperwidth x \paperheight 614.295pt x 794.97pt
//   columns (banal)       2 (the kit's appendix is \onecolumn; banal still says 2)
//   \textwidth            487.8225pt = 6.75 in        \columnsep 18.07pt = 0.25 in
//   \textheight           650.43pt = 9.0 in
//   body size             \f@size 10, \baselineskip 12pt (the kit says 11: the style redefines
//                         \@normalsize, which LaTeX2e no longer calls); banal 10.3
//   references            \normalsize (the style sets no \bibfont): banal reports no reference
//                         size; with \bibfont \small, banal 9.3
//   fonts (pdf.js)        NimbusRomNo9L-Regu / -Medi / -ReguItal / -MediItal (ptm), NimbusMonL-Regu
//                         (\ttdefault pcr), Computer Modern math — Type 1, embedded. And
//                         Times-Roman, NOT embedded, from the kit's own figure icml_numpapers.pdf
//                         (in the kit's example_paper.pdf too): pdf/fonts reports it
//
// The planted defects, measured on the same example: `[11pt]` → banal 11.3, pdf/body-size;
// `\renewcommand{\rmdefault}{cmr}` → Computer Modern, pdf/fonts; a bibliography in \small → banal
// 9.3, pdf/body-size; NeurIPS 2025's style instead of ICML's → 1 column, pdf/geometry. What this
// family does NOT see, measured: `\usepackage[margin=0.6in]{geometry}` gave \textwidth 527.57pt with
// the same page, columns, fonts and body size, and no pdf/* rule reported it (no rule reads the text
// block; format/layout-override runs only under a `template`). The style itself prints «The page
// layout violates the ICML style.» on page 1 of such a build. `[a4paper]` changes nothing: the style
// forces letter.
//
// Its card, in prose: `icml-style.md` beside this file.
{
  // A template family: it names no venue, so no `name` and no `url` (the venues that extend it do).
  "type": "family",

  // TeX Live packages the style and the kit's example need ON TOP OF tex-base.jsonc. icml2027.sty
  // itself travels with the paper. Measured 2026-11-19: every file the example's build read (219,
  // pdflatex's -recorder list) mapped to its package through texlive.tlpdb.
  //   eso-pic · forloop — the style's \RequirePackage; paperlint's cache had neither, and the build
  //     stopped at «File `eso-pic.sty' not found».
  //   psnfss · times · courier · helvetic — \RequirePackage{times}: Times for the text, Courier for
  //     \ttfamily (pcrr7t.tfm read), Helvetica for \sffamily (\sfdefault phv).
  //   fancyhdr · algorithms · float — the kit ships its own fancyhdr.sty, algorithm.sty and
  //     algorithmic.sty; a paper without them builds from TeX Live's, with the same facts
  //     (measured). algorithm.sty loads float.
  //   amscls · mathtools · cleveref · todonotes — not the style's: example_paper.tex loads amsthm,
  //     mathtools, cleveref and todonotes, and a paper started from the kit keeps them.
  "tex": {
    "packages": {
      "eso-pic": ["eso-pic.sty"],
      "forloop": ["forloop.sty"],
      "psnfss": ["times.sty", "ot1ptm.fd"],
      "times": ["ptmr7t.tfm", "utmr8a.pfb"],
      "courier": ["pcrr7t.tfm"],
      "helvetic": ["phvr7t.tfm"],
      "fancyhdr": ["fancyhdr.sty"],
      "algorithms": ["algorithm.sty", "algorithmic.sty"],
      "float": ["float.sty"],
      "amscls": ["amsthm.sty"],
      "mathtools": ["mathtools.sty"],
      "cleveref": ["cleveref.sty"],
      "todonotes": ["todonotes.sty"],
    },
  },

  "format": {
    // «Your paper must be in US letter size (i.e., not A4 or other sizes).»
    // (https://icml.cc/Conferences/2027/AuthorInstructions, read 2026-11-19); banal: 8.5 x 11 in.
    "page_size": "letter",
    "page_w_in": 8.5,
    "page_h_in": 11.0,

    // «an overall width of 6.75 inches, height of 9.0 inches» (the kit); \textwidth and \textheight
    // in the log, above.
    "textblock_w_in": 6.75,
    "textblock_h_in": 9.0,

    // «formatted in two columns» (the kit); banal: 2.
    "columns": 2,

    // «10 point Times font» (the kit); banal measures the rendered mode, 10.3. The 0.5 tolerance is
    // the other families': banal reads 0.3 pt high on every template measured.
    "body_pt": 10,
    "body_pt_tol": 0.5,

    // «The citation font size should be the same as that in the main body of the paper.» (the
    // author instructions above, camera-ready section). 10 pt; body_pt_tol widens it to 9.5-10.5, so
    // a bibliography in \small (banal 9.3) is reported. A bibliography at the body's size is given no
    // reference size by banal, so it is not judged.
    "ref_pt_min": 10,
    "ref_pt_max": 10,

    // The Times clone. The title and headings are bold Times, so a `fonts_title` would repeat this
    // check and report a Computer Modern build twice.
    "fonts_text": "NimbusRomNo9L",
  },
}
